            SortOrder = 1,
            AppRoutes = """["/accounting","/admin/capabilities"]""",
            Tags = """["accounting","general-ledger","finance","setup"]""",
            ContentJson = """{"body":"## The Full General Ledger\n\nOut of the box, Forge keeps the books that a shop needs to run: invoices, payments, bills, vendor payments, aging and a set of financial reports built from those records. That is the standalone mode the other Accounting modules describe. Many shops never need more, and shops that connect QuickBooks or another accounting system keep the real ledger there.\n\nThe **full general ledger** is the third option. It is a complete double-entry ledger that lives inside Forge: a chart of accounts you control, manual journal entries, an append-only register of every posting, budgets, period and year-end close, and bank reconciliation against the cash accounts in your own books. It is built in and ready, but it is switched **off** by default, so the screens that write to it do not appear until an admin turns it on.\n\n### What you get when it is on\n\nThe Accounting home page gains three tiles that are only there with the full ledger:\n\n- **Chart of Accounts** — create and maintain your own accounts.\n- **New Journal Entry** — compose and post a balanced manual entry.\n- **Budgets** — enter a budget per income and expense account and compare it to actual.\n\nThe tiles you already know — Ledger, Trial Balance, Profit & Loss, Balance Sheet, Cash Flow, AR and AP Aging, Period Close, Bank Reconciliation, Bank Statements, CPA Exports — keep working, but now they are fed by real postings in your own ledger rather than by summaries computed from operational records. Every invoice, payment, bill, receipt and payroll run posts a journal entry automatically; the manual entry screen is for everything else.\n\n### Turning it on\n\nOnly an admin can switch the full ledger on, under **Admin > Capabilities**. Two things have to be true first.\n\n1. **Forge is the accounting system of record.** The full ledger cannot be on at the same time as a connected external accounting provider. If your shop pushes to QuickBooks, that connection stays and the full ledger stays off.\n2. **Opening balances are posted.** Before the first day the ledger is live, your accountant loads the closing trial balance from the old books as an opening journal, and checks the tie-out so the two agree to the penny. Forge refuses to switch the full ledger on until that opening journal is posted for every book. This is deliberate: a ledger that starts from zero on a random Tuesday can never be reconciled to anything.\n\nThe practical order is: agree a go-live date with your accountant, close the old books through the day before it, have the opening balances posted, then have the admin flip the switch. From that day forward every transaction in Forge posts to the ledger.\n\n### Turning it off\n\nIf the full ledger is ever switched off again, nothing is deleted. The ledger, chart of accounts, trial balance and statements stay viewable and exportable, so your history is still there for the auditor or the tax preparer. What goes away is the ability to post: the Chart of Accounts, New Journal Entry and Budgets tiles disappear, and the automatic postings stop.\n\n### Rules the ledger keeps for you\n\n- **Every entry balances.** Debits must equal credits before Forge will post, and the editor will not let you post an unbalanced entry.\n- **Nothing is edited after posting.** A posted entry is permanent. To undo it you post a reversal; to fix it you post a correction. The original stays on the register as evidence.\n- **Closed periods are closed.** Once a period is hard-closed, nothing can post into it. A soft close can be reopened; a hard close cannot.\n- **Structural fields lock once used.** An account's number, type and normal balance cannot change after it has postings. Create a new account instead.\n\n### Where to go next\n\n- *Chart of Accounts* — setting up and maintaining accounts.\n- *Journal Entries and the Ledger* — posting, reversing, correcting and finding entries.\n- *Period Close and Year-End* — soft close, hard close and the retained-earnings roll.\n- *Bank Reconciliation* — matching the cash account to the bank statement.\n- *Budgets* — budget entry and budget vs actual.\n\nIf you want to practice first, the **Training** tile on the Accounting home page opens a sandbox ledger where nothing you post touches the real books.","sections":[]}"""
        });

        await GetOrCreateModule(new TrainingModule
