            SortOrder = 2,
            AppRoutes = """["/accounting/chart-of-accounts"]""",
            Tags = """["accounting","general-ledger","chart-of-accounts"]""",
            ContentJson = """{"body":"## Chart of Accounts\n\nThe chart of accounts is the list of every account the ledger can post to. Open it from the **Chart of Accounts** tile on the Accounting home page. The table shows each account's number, name, type, whether it is postable and whether it is active. Click a row (or its **Edit** button) to open the editor; **New Account** in the toolbar starts a blank one.\n\n### The five types\n\nEvery account is one of **Asset**, **Liability**, **Equity**, **Income** or **Expense**. The type decides which statement the account lands on: assets, liabilities and equity go to the Balance Sheet; income and expense go to the Profit & Loss and are what the year-end close rolls into retained earnings.\n\n### Normal balance\n\nEach account has a **Normal Balance** of Debit or Credit. Assets and expenses normally carry a debit balance; liabilities, equity and income normally carry a credit balance. Forge uses this to show balances as positive numbers on the reports, so pick it to match the type. A contra account (accumulated depreciation, for example) is the exception: an asset with a credit normal balance.\n\n### Numbering and parents\n\nAccount numbers are yours to choose, but keep a scheme. The common one is 1000s for assets, 2000s for liabilities, 3000s for equity, 4000s for income and 5000s and up for expenses. **Parent Account** lets you nest: an account with a parent rolls up under it on the statements, which is how you get a single Utilities line made of separate Gas, Electric and Water accounts.\n\n### Control accounts\n\nSome accounts carry a **Control** badge and cannot be edited from this screen. These are the accounts the automatic postings rely on: accounts receivable, accounts payable, inventory, cash, sales tax payable and the like. Forge manages them so that the sub-ledgers always tie to the general ledger. Post to them through the operational screens (invoices, bills, payments), not by hand.\n\n### The other fields\n\n- **Cash Flow Category** — Operating, Investing or Financing. This is how the account is grouped on the Cash Flow statement. Leave it as None for accounts that do not belong on it.\n- **Requires Job** — every line posted to this account must name a job. Use it on cost-of-goods and work-in-process accounts so nothing lands there without a job to carry it.\n- **Requires Cost Center** — every line must name a cost center. Useful on overhead expense accounts when you run activity-based costing.\n- **Active** — turn this off to retire an account. Inactive accounts stay on the reports for their history but are no longer offered when posting.\n- **Description** — a note for the next bookkeeper about what belongs here.\n\n### What locks\n\nOnce an account has a single posting, its **Account Number**, **Type** and **Normal Balance** are locked and the editor shows a lock note. Name, parent, cash flow category, the two Requires toggles, Active and Description stay editable. If you need a structural change, create a new account and retire the old one.\n\n### Setting up for the first time\n\nYour accountant's opening trial balance is the best starting list: every account with a balance on it needs to exist here before the opening journal is posted. Add the accounts you will post to during the year even if they open at zero, so you are not creating accounts in the middle of entering a bill.","sections":[]}"""
