            AppRoutes = """["/accounting/journal-entries/new","/accounting/ledger"]""",
            Tags = """["accounting","general-ledger","journal-entries","ledger"]""",
            ContentJson = """{"body":"## Journal Entries and the Ledger\n\nMost postings in the full ledger happen on their own. Post an invoice and the ledger gets a receivable and a sale; record a payment and it gets cash and a reduced receivable. The **New Journal Entry** screen is for the entries that no operational screen produces: accruals, depreciation, prepaid amortization, owner contributions, corrections your accountant asks for at month end.\n\n### Composing an entry\n\nThe editor has a header and a list of lines.\n\n- **Entry date** — the date the entry belongs to. It must fall in an open period; a soft-closed or hard-closed period will refuse it.\n- **Memo** — a short description of why the entry exists. Required. Write it for the person reading the ledger a year from now.\n- **Lines** — at least two. Each line names an **Account**, a **Debit** or a **Credit** (not both), and an optional **Description** for that line alone. **Add line** appends another; the trash icon removes one, but the editor keeps a minimum of two.\n\nThe bar under the lines shows the running **Debit** and **Credit** totals and either **Balanced** or **Out of balance**. **Post entry** stays disabled until the totals match, the date and memo are filled in, and every line has an account and an amount. Once posted, the entry appears on the ledger with its own entry number and cannot be changed.\n\n### The Ledger register\n\nThe **Ledger** tile opens the register: every posted entry, newest first, with its entry number, date, source, memo and running balance. Expand a row to see its lines. Each line's account is a link; click it to open the ledger *lens* for that account, showing only that account's entries with a balance after each one. **All entries** takes you back to the full register.\n\n**Source** tells you where an entry came from — an invoice, a payment, a bill, payroll, the opening conversion, a manual entry, a reversal — which is the first thing to check when a number looks wrong.\n\n### Finding an entry\n\nThe **Find in ledger** box in the page header searches the loaded register. Type a memo fragment, an amount or an account and the count shows *1 of 4*; press Enter or use the arrows to step between matches, and the register scrolls to and highlights each one.\n\n### Reverse vs correct\n\nBecause posted entries never change, there are two ways to fix a mistake, both from the expanded row.\n\n- **Reverse** posts an equal-and-opposite entry on the date you choose, with a required reason. The original stays on the register and both entries link to each other (*Reverses #12* / *Reversed by #15*). Use this when the entry should never have happened.\n- **Correct (new entry)** opens the editor pre-filled from the original so you can post the right version. The memo starts with *Correction of entry #12* so the trail is obvious. Use this when the entry was right in principle but wrong in an account or an amount — usually after reversing the original.\n\nThe Reverse button is only offered on a posted entry that has not already been reversed.\n\n### Review tools\n\nTwo helpers sit on the register for month-end review. **Scan for anomalies** looks over the loaded entries for two classic red flags — a manual entry posted straight to a control account (receivables, payables, inventory, cash should move through their sub-ledgers) and a manual entry at or above the large-entry review threshold — and flags those rows for you to look at; the flags are advice, not verdicts. Inside an expanded row, **Explain with AI** writes a plain-language summary of what the entry did. If the assistant is offline you still get a plain summary.\n\n### Habits that keep the ledger clean\n\n1. Let the operational screens post whenever they can. Manual entries to control accounts (receivables, payables, inventory, cash) make the sub-ledgers drift from the ledger.\n2. Memo every manual entry with the *why*, not just the *what*.\n3. Reverse first, then correct, rather than posting a one-sided fix on top.\n4. Date entries in the period they belong to, not the day you found the problem.","sections":[]}"""
