            AppRoutes = """["/accounting/bank-rec","/accounting/bank-statements"]""",
            Tags = """["accounting","general-ledger","bank-reconciliation","cash"]""",
            ContentJson = """{"body":"## Bank Reconciliation\n\nA bank reconciliation proves that the cash account in your ledger and the balance the bank reports are the same money, once you allow for checks that have not cleared and deposits in transit. With the full ledger on, the **Bank Reconciliation** page does this against your own cash accounts, one statement at a time.\n\n### Starting one\n\nAt the top of the page, **Start a reconciliation** asks for three things:\n\n- **Cash account** — which bank account in the ledger you are reconciling.\n- **Statement date** — the closing date printed on the bank statement.\n- **Ending balance** — the closing balance printed on the statement.\n\nClick **Start** and Forge opens a worksheet in **Draft** status. Below the form, **Reconciliations** lists every worksheet, draft or finalized, so you can reopen a draft or look back at a finished month.\n\n### The worksheet\n\nThe summary table shows four numbers:\n\n- **Book (GL) cash balance** — what the ledger says the account holds as of the statement date.\n- **Statement ending balance** — what you typed from the statement.\n- **Cleared** — the total of the lines you have marked as cleared.\n- **Outstanding** — the lines not yet cleared: checks the bank has not paid, deposits it has not credited.\n\nThe chip in the page header shows either **Reconciled** or **Difference** with the amount still unexplained. The goal is a difference of exactly zero.\n\nUnder the summary is the list of cash lines from the ledger that have not been cleared by an earlier reconciliation. Tick the **Cleared** box on every line that appears on the bank statement. Ticking only stages the change — the unsaved count appears next to the **Update cleared** button — and nothing is recorded until you click **Update cleared**. The totals recompute when you save.\n\nIf you imported the statement through **Bank Statements** and confirmed the auto-match suggestions there, the matched lines arrive here already cleared, and the reconciliation is mostly a matter of checking what is left.\n\n### Getting to zero\n\nWhen the difference is not zero after everything on the statement is ticked, the usual culprits are:\n\n- A bank fee, interest or a charge on the statement that was never entered. Post it as a journal entry dated in the period, come back, and tick it.\n- A deposit recorded in the ledger as one amount but taken by the bank as two, or the other way around.\n- A typo in the ending balance you entered when starting.\n- A line ticked by mistake. Untick it and save again.\n\n### Finalizing\n\n**Finalize** is enabled only when the difference is zero and there are no unsaved changes. It locks the worksheet, marks the cleared lines as reconciled so they never appear on a later worksheet, and sets the status to **Finalized**. A finalized reconciliation cannot be edited; if you find a problem later, fix it with journal entries in the next period and it will show up on the next reconciliation.\n\nReconcile every cash account every month before you soft-close the period. It is the cheapest control you have, and the first thing an auditor asks for.","sections":[]}"""
        });

